## Manager consolidation acceptance and deployment-proof correction

The consolidation boundary is accepted. Verified `develop..HEAD` contains exactly:
- `3d6c421743e3d5249aa773423f4e04df9e2792c8` — atomic command-service code integration, 93 paths, with no `project/` or excluded paths.
- `4b4e5872e19deed3daf883861f817e4f923bfeb8` — Kanbus-generated `project/` paths only.

The remaining non-project worktree changes are exactly the three accepted exclusions. The prior focused validation remains applicable because consolidation changed history/commit grouping, not content.

### Deployment-proof correction
The task description’s “retained sandbox” wording is now stale. The committed backend intentionally omits the ECS command service from Amplify sandboxes; a sandbox can only exercise the explicitly separate legacy proof harness and therefore cannot prove the integrated ECS path. The live non-production proof for this integration must use **staging**, after a manager-reviewed readiness gate. Production remains prohibited.

### Next authorized slice — read-only staging readiness gate
Continue on `plx-925843`; do not create a new task. Inspect, without deployment or source changes:
- current AWS identity, region, Amplify app/branch mapping, and staging environment selection;
- existence and consistency of the staging command-service foundation contract (VPC/subnets, ECR repository, image and Task-table SSM state);
- deployment-role ARN and its narrow bootstrap/activity-gate requirements;
- runtime config secret presence and required key names without reading or recording secret values;
- immutable image build/handoff prerequisites, current active-Task gate state, and rollback/deferred-image behavior;
- the exact foundation/application deployment sequence and bounded staging proof/rollback commands.

Return a sanitized go/no-go checklist with any missing prerequisite, exact proposed commands, expected mutations, rollback points, and proof cases. Do not deploy, push, open a PR, modify code, stage/commit, close the task, or start the extended-duration follow-up.
